'      ,q''[    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="Flow_11z8xs6" sourceRef="Gateway_19h24z4" targetRef="Activity_Concept3" apex:sequence="10" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_1fvohcn">]''',
unistr('      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>How to Navigate through a Gateway for a Demo Model:\00A0]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Method 1: Default Routing.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Note that the Lower Route has a ''/'' mark across the flow.\00A0 This denotes it as the default path.]'''),
'      ,q''[To add default path:]''',
'      ,q''[- select the Flow between the Gateway and the next object.]''',
'      ,q''[- click on the ''morph'' tool (the spanner)]''',
'      ,q''[- select default path</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_0jg4oqm" sourceRef="Gateway_One" targetRef="TextAnnotation_1fvohcn" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_0qhtqn5">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>This is a mini-tutorial on Gateways that will help you create a test model here and execute it.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[When you add Flows for APEX into your application, you would have some application logic helping to steer you through the gateways.\00A0 In this demo environment, we have to cheat a little.]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[The first gateway pair uses default routing to force your model to always go on one path.\00A0 Obviously, to test your model you can edit the model to move the default from one path to another....]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[The second gateway uses Gateway Routing Expressions (new in v22.2) - which allows you to define an expression on each of the forward paths. \00A0These are evaluated in sequence_number order - and the first path with an expression that evaluates')
||unistr(' to ''true'' is chosen. \00A0]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[The third gateway pair shows you how a special process variable, called a Gateway Routing Variable, \00A0can be used to instruct the gateway which route to take. \00A0This is the ''old way'' to set gateway routing -- but sometimes this can still be u')
||'seful.</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_1y1u9dd" sourceRef="Activity_Concept1" targetRef="TextAnnotation_0qhtqn5" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_1vzs22f">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>Method 3: Instructing the Gateway using Routing Variables]''',
unistr('      ,q''[(This is useful - but a bit more\00A0complicated!)]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Click on the Gateway &amp; see that it has an ID of ''Gateway_Three'' in the properties panel on the right.]''',
'      ,q''[Note that the two forward paths have ID''s of ''Flow_3_Yes'' and ''Flow_3_No'']''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[To instruct our gateway ''Gateway_Three'' which way to route the process, you would set up a Process Variable with the routing instructions somewhere in your application before you get to the Gateway.\00A0]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[You need to set a process variable with name = &lt;gateway_ID&gt;&lt;:route&gt;. The value needs to be the ID of the route you want it to take.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[So in our example, we need to set process variable ''Gateway_Three:route'' with a varchar2 value of ''Flow_3_Yes'' to go down the 3_Yes route, and ''Flow_3_No'' to go down the 3_No route.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[In the Flow Monitor, you can set this manually or you can use the Gateway Route Tool. Run a copy of this model in Flow Control. Before you get to Gateway Three, Click on the Variables Tab. Add a row into the Interactive Grid to create our p'
||'rocess variable named ''Gateway_Three:route'' , give it a type of VARCHAR2, and set its value to ''Flow_3_No''. Don''t forget to Save this. Then go to the Subflows tab, and step the process on through the gateway.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Using the Gateway Routing Tool makes this manual process easier. Once you have the process running, select the Variables tab. This gives you a pop-up list of opening gateways, and shows you the possible route options. Select the one or ones'
||' you want, and you will see the process variable has been created and set for you. Again, don''t forget to save this, then go to the Subflows tab, and step the process on through the gateway.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[In a real application you wouldn''t do this through the Flow Engine console. Somewhere in your application you would set the routing based on your data. This could be a variable set call in any previous step, or you could have a scriptTask i'
||unistr('n the step immediately before that runs a PL/SQL script to set it based on some query or whatever your imagination can create, or you could use a declarative variable expression to set this in the gateway step.\00A0 We''ll explore that later...</bpmn:text')
||'>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_0hx891w" sourceRef="Activity_Concept3" targetRef="TextAnnotation_'))
);
