'      ,q''[        </bpmn:extensionElements>]''',
'      ,q''[      </bpmn:timerEventDefinition>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:intermediateCatchEvent>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:endEvent id="Event_1p9psxw">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:incoming>Flow_1xtmf5p</bpmn:incoming>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:endEvent>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="Flow_1xtmf5p" sourceRef="Event_Oracle_Time" targetRef="Event_1p9psxw" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:intermediateCatchEvent id="Event_ISO_Date" name="ISO Date">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:documentation>This uses an ISO 8601 date Time String to specify a fixed future time.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[See documentation for ISO 8601 Time specs - or Google ''ISO 8601''.]''',
'      ,q''[</bpmn:documentation>]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:incoming>Flow_0exp2j4</bpmn:incoming>]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:outgoing>Flow_083i9d5</bpmn:outgoing>]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:timerEventDefinition id="TimerEventDefinition_0kqfp2i">]''',
'      ,q''[        <bpmn:timeDate xsi:type="bpmn:tFormalExpression">20250901T13:30:20</bpmn:timeDate>]''',
'      ,q''[      </bpmn:timerEventDefinition>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:intermediateCatchEvent>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:endEvent id="Event_0dozg9s">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:incoming>Flow_083i9d5</bpmn:incoming>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:endEvent>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="Flow_083i9d5" sourceRef="Event_ISO_Date" targetRef="Event_0dozg9s" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_0b87nxd">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>If you want to make your process wait for some period of time before it continues, you can add a Timer.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[To add a Timer, start by adding an Intermediate Event (the double circle), then change it with the Spanner Tool to an Intermediate Timer Catch Event.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[A timer delay / pause can be specified as either:]''',
unistr('      ,q''[\00A0- a DURATION -- how long you want to pause the process \00A0(e.g., wait for 2 Hours),or\00A0]'''),
unistr('      ,q''[\00A0- a TIME that the process flow should wait before restarting \00A0(e.g., wait until 31-DEC-2025 23:59:59 ).]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[This First Timer is set for 10 seconds. \00A0Move onto the timer event. \00A0 \00A0After a few seconds, select the Refresh option on the Actions menu to refresh the screen. \00A0 The timer actually checks to see which processes should proceed every 10 seco')
||unistr('nds* -- so you might need to wait unto an additional 10 seconds for the timer to fire. \00A0 If it still hasn''t fired after 30* seconds, timers might not be enabled currently on your system - ask to your system administrator or read the documentation on ')
||'setting up theScheduler...]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[* The interval at which timers are checked would typically be 10 seconds on a production system - but you can see how your system is set up if you Click on Configuration - Timers. \00A0On public demo systems like apex.oracle.com (and apex.oracl')
||'ecorp.com) you will have to wait a bit longer - the timer checking interval is every minute.</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_07h0he2" sourceRef="Activity_16kndlc" targetRef="TextAnnotation_0b87nxd" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_0pb1xw1">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>This is set up using IS0 8601 format for a 10 Second delay</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_1ex81lj" sourceRef="Event_0s12ekd" targetRef="TextAnnotation_0pb1xw1" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_0uwfxws">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>15 Seconds delay.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[The formatting here is important....</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_09dzds0" sourceRef="Event_Ora_Duration" targetRef="TextAnnotation_0uwfxws" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_1defw8d">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>ISO 8601 Duration.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Durations start with P (for period).]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Then you could add 2M3W4D for 2 Months, 3 Weeks and 4 Days.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Then comes a T for Time]''',
'      ,q''[Then something like 2H20M15S]''',
'      ,q''[- for 2 Hours, 20 Minutes and 15 seconds.</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_1q8z07t" sourceRef="Event_ISO_Duration" targetRef="TextAnnotation_1defw8d" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_15qukwy">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>You can specify an Oracle Date (optionally including a time) along with the format mask used for that date.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[In practicing, hard coding a fixed Date into your model is not often very useful.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[If you specify Just a Time (no date), the timer will go off next time that timer occurs (later today, or tomorrow if the time has already passed today).]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Or you can use substitution of a process variable --- more on that later in the tutorials....</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_0073qme" sourceRef="Event_Oracle_Time" targetRef="TextAnnotation_15qukwy" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_1grdtja">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>This uses an ISO 8601 date Time String to specify a fixed future time.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[See documentation for ISO 8601 Time specs - or Google ''ISO 8601''.</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_0xnxta8" sourceRef="Event_ISO_Date" targetRef="TextAnnotation_1grdtja" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_0jw7law">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>We''ll cover more advanced uses of timers in later sections, including Tutorial 4d on Timer Boundary Events.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[You can also add a Timer to a process Start Event if you want to delay it''s start.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[We''ll also look at how to make a process wait for a message from another process or from an outside program in Message Flow...</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_1c34puf" sourceRef="Event_0dozg9s" targetRef="TextAnnotation_0jw7law" />]''',
