'    pi_dgrm_name => ''Tutorial 3b - Substitution and Bind Syntax'',',
'    pi_dgrm_short_description => ''Flows for APEX BPMN Tutorial - part 3b - discusses how to use substitution and  bind syntax to reference the content of a process variable.  This tutorial is not executable - just read the model...'',',
'    pi_dgrm_description => ''The business process model titled "Tutorial 3b - Substitution and Bind Syntax" is a structured representation of a workflow tutorial intended to educate users on the usage of substitution and bind syntax within a process m'
||'anagement system, specifically designed for Oracle APEX (Application Express) environments aligned with BPMN (Business Process Model and Notation) standards.',
'',
'#### Key Components:',
'',
'**Process Variables**:',
'',
'*   The beginning of the tutorial emphasizes how process variables are utilized, which are crucial for programming dynamic and adaptable business processes.',
'',
'**Syntax Utilization**:',
'',
'*   **Substitution Syntax**: Introduces substitution of process variables into expressions, with details on using the `F4A$`prefix. This syntax is used for character formatting of a variable directly into a string.',
'*   **Bind Syntax**: Binds variables directly into expressions or scripts using a similar prefix. It emphasizes safety and prevention against SQL injection, recommending usage in scripts to avoid unnecessary data conversion.',
'',
'**Workflow and Gateways**:',
'',
'*   Incorporates various tasks and decision gateways (e.g., parallel and exclusive gateways) to demonstrate process flow control using binding and substitution in different scenarios (e.g., tasks, gateways, user tasks).',
'*   It represents timing and conditional pathways using intermediate events and sequence flows.',
'',
'**Conceptual Segments**:',
'',
'*   Presents three key concepts: the practical utilization of process variables, substitution vs. bind syntax nuances, and vital considerations on data formats (e.g., timestamps, dates).',
'',
'**Practical Advice**:',
'',
'*   Offers advice on the use of substitutions primarily for VARCHAR2 formatting and warns about proper formatting when handling dates and timestamps.',
'*   Shows how binds should be preferred for direct variable usage and security reasons.',
'',
'**Annotations & Documentation**:',
'',
'*   The model is supported by text annotations clarifying various points on syntax usage and helping users understand the context without requiring the execution of the tutorial model itself.',
'',
'**End-End Process**:',
'',
'*   Ensures practitioners understand that the tutorial''''s goal is to educate about reading and understanding the model rather than executing it.',
'',
'This model serves as an educational tool to help IT professionals and developers working in the Oracle APEX environment enhance their understanding of dynamic data handling using BPMN, which can improve their efficiency in developing robust, secure a'
||'pplications.'',',
