'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_1xxo0g1">]''',
unistr('      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>Tutorial Section 5: \00A0Introduction to SubProcesses and Call Activities]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Both Sub Processes and Call Activities allow you to break your process down into subprocesses. \00A0with bpmn:subProcesses, those sub Processes are defined as part of your diagram and are only callable from within the diagram they are contained')
||unistr(' on. \00A0]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[CallActivities allow you to call a different diagram as the detail of your subProcess. \00A0This allows you to reuse process components across different processes. \00A0 For example, a bank could define a model for its ''Know Your Client'' process. \00A0')
||' This process could then be built into many other process diagrams -- as a call - rather than being contained in each diagram.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[The tutorials in this section look at some of the details of Sub Processes and Call activities...</bpmn:text>]''',
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_1i1gbpg" sourceRef="Event_0n10060" targetRef="TextAnnotation_1xxo0g1" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_1uz56tw">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>This is a SubProcess]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Once the subProcess becomes the current task on the main path, processing starts inside the subProcess -- starting at its Start event. \00A0When The Sub Process is complete, processing returns to the main path.]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Sub Processes can have Boundary Events set on them -- so just like a Task could have a Reminder or a TimeOut Set with Timer Boundary Events (see Tutorial 2d), so can a Sub Procedure. \00A0We''ll look at other Boundary Events in Tutorial 4c.]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[A Sub Process can only be accessed from the diagram that contains it.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[This SubProcess is ''expanded'' - you can see all of the details of the subProcess from the main diagram. \00A0The next subprocess in Concet 2 shows a ''collapsed'' SubProcess.</bpmn:text>]'''),
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_07bb3i9" sourceRef="Activity_0jjqteg" targetRef="TextAnnotation_1uz56tw" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_0u7euj0">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>This is a Call Activity]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[A Call activity is a link to another Process Diagram.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[When the Call Activity becomes the current object in the workflow, the process then executes the process defined in that other diagram; \00A0 When the called diagram is complete, the process returns to the calling diagram and continues.]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Adding Call Activities allows processes to be shared and reused by other processes. \00A0 We''ll look into this in more detail in Tutorial 5d...</bpmn:text>]'''),
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:association id="Association_03losbj" sourceRef="Activity_1xsihhy" targetRef="TextAnnotation_0u7euj0" />]''',
'      ,q''[    <bpmn:textAnnotation id="TextAnnotation_0u2xfsn">]''',
'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>Sub Processes can have their detail hidden in a ''Collapsed SubProcess''.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Click on the Blue Arrow Widget to open the SubProcess Details...]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Before v23.1, we discouraged use of Collapsed SubProcesses because it was hard to see the detail. \00A0 Now that you are able to drill down into the subprocess, they are a very useful tool to keep your models de-cluttered - and probably a bette')
||'r way to go!]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Collapsed SubProcesses can run multiple times, once for each item in a collection. \00A0See Tutorial 7f on Iterations and Loops (requires Enterprise Edition).</bpmn:text>]'''),
'      ,q''[    </bpmn:textAnnotation>]''',
