'      ,q''[      <bpmn:text>Tutorial 6 - Introducing Escalations and Error Events]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Let''s build on our Sub Process Model.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Escalations are used when a process has a warning, or something happens when you need to bring in an extra process to help.\00A0]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[We use an Escalation Intermediate Throw Event between B3 and B6 to signal that we have an escalation.\00A0The escalation is caught by the Non-Interrupting Escalation Boundary Event on the sub process boundary, where our escalation handling proc')
||unistr('ess is defined.\00A0This is non-interrupting - so the main process continues.\00A0]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Escalations can be non-interrupting (usual) or can interrupt the process.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Sub process B4 has an Error End Event - so in this tutorial it always ends with an error - which interrupts the sub-process, and passes control to the error handler if you have one defined.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[Note that we''re talking about business process Escalations (and Errors) here - not technical escalations (or errors!).]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[Error Events and Escalation Events can be used to send Errors and Escalations from inside a SubProcess or CallActivity to their Calling Process Level. \00A0They are only used with SubProcesses and CallActivities.]'''),
'      ,q''[]''',
'      ,q''[So here the Error End Event (Pink) throws an Error to the Error Boundary Event (Pink) resulting in SubProcess B4 taking a different End path if an error is signalled.]''',
'      ,q''[]''',
unistr('      ,q''[And the Escalation Throw Event (Purple) in SubProcess B throws an escalation which is caught by the non-interrupting escalation catch event on the outside of SubProcess B, (Purple) - causing the\00A0]'''),
'      ,q''[purple path to execute</bpmn:text>]''',
